00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:23.041
So. Yeah.

00:01:23.041 --> 00:01:24.375
And the sense of ensemble.

00:01:24.375 --> 00:01:24.667
Yeah.

00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:25.585
Everybody is working up

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:27.796
about telling the story and not about,

00:01:27.796 --> 00:01:29.380
you know, standing out in a room.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:30.298
Throwing the ball.

00:01:30.298 --> 00:01:33.176
That's right. You mentioned the costumes.

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:34.719
The esthetics of fashion

00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:35.845
is not my specialty,

00:01:35.845 --> 00:01:37.639
but you can't possibly

00:01:37.639 --> 00:01:39.766
ignore the extraordinary costumes,

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:40.892
how they all come together.

00:01:40.892 --> 00:01:43.645
The set is also unbelievable.

00:01:43.645 --> 00:01:45.688
But Morgan, the characters as well,

00:01:45.688 --> 00:01:47.440
some of them are directly

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.400
at a adaptation's from real people.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:50.944
Some of them are

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:51.778
akin to people

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:53.321
or have through and through.

00:01:53.321 --> 00:01:54.697
How do you prepare for that,

00:01:54.697 --> 00:01:56.074
knowing that there's a historic parallel

00:01:56.074 --> 00:01:57.867
to who you're playing?

00:01:57.867 --> 00:02:00.745
The the the, the, the real sort of analog

00:02:00.745 --> 00:02:01.663
that I was given

00:02:01.663 --> 00:02:02.831
initially with Jay Gould.

00:02:02.831 --> 00:02:04.040
So I,

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:05.291
I read a bunch about him

00:02:05.291 --> 00:02:06.042
and he's a he's

00:02:06.042 --> 00:02:07.544
a really fascinating character.

00:02:07.544 --> 00:02:09.087
I mean, a lot of these people,

00:02:09.087 --> 00:02:10.755
this sort of American myth that you can,

00:02:10.755 --> 00:02:10.964
you know,

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:11.965
you can pull yourself up

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:12.841
by your bootstraps

00:02:12.841 --> 00:02:13.716
or you can sort of turn your,

00:02:13.716 --> 00:02:15.009
you know, the rags to riches stories.

00:02:15.009 --> 00:02:15.844
And these guys really were

00:02:15.844 --> 00:02:16.386
that, you know, these

00:02:16.386 --> 00:02:17.846
these were these were tough.

00:02:17.846 --> 00:02:19.931
These were hard, man.

00:02:19.931 --> 00:02:21.432
But it's such a family man.

00:02:21.432 --> 00:02:22.934
And he, you know, like to ten is orchids.

00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:23.351
But yes.

00:02:23.351 --> 00:02:26.437
And he was so he had that duality of of

00:02:26.604 --> 00:02:28.148
of being sort of truly

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:29.816
ruthless in business,

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:31.109
but loving and tender

00:02:31.109 --> 00:02:33.069
in his domestic life.

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:35.321
I think what's fascinating to me is

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:37.115
you guys are able to thread together

00:02:37.115 --> 00:02:39.784
the dynamics of race and class and gender

00:02:39.784 --> 00:02:41.077
and labor disputes, things

00:02:41.077 --> 00:02:43.163
that are obviously still very much issues

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:43.872
today.

00:02:43.872 --> 00:02:45.915
It's subtle, it's nuanced

00:02:45.915 --> 00:02:46.875
and it

00:02:46.875 --> 00:02:47.792
it doesn't feel like

00:02:47.792 --> 00:02:49.335
a blunt, in-your-face. This is the issue.

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:51.462
You realize that it's all together

00:02:51.462 --> 00:02:53.798
threaded in that time period.

00:02:53.798 --> 00:02:55.216
I think Julian Fellowes really knows

00:02:55.216 --> 00:02:56.176
his audience.

00:02:56.176 --> 00:02:57.010
I think he knows that

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.303
they want to be entertained.

00:02:58.303 --> 00:02:59.762
They're coming for the costumes.

00:02:59.762 --> 00:03:02.140
They're coming for The Real Housewives,

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:03.641
you know, style disputes.

00:03:03.641 --> 00:03:06.728
And he's also then able to enter the time

00:03:06.728 --> 00:03:07.854
period obliquely and sort of

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:09.314
bring in these other factors

00:03:09.314 --> 00:03:10.106
that were sort of bubbling

00:03:10.106 --> 00:03:10.732
under the surface.

00:03:10.732 --> 00:03:11.441
You know, we're not

00:03:11.441 --> 00:03:12.275
we're not dealing

00:03:12.275 --> 00:03:13.943
with the complexity of the,

00:03:13.943 --> 00:03:16.029
you know, economic disparity necessarily.

00:03:16.029 --> 00:03:16.988
But he is sort of

00:03:16.988 --> 00:03:18.406
tiptoeing into all those worlds.

00:03:18.406 --> 00:03:19.407
So people are there.

00:03:19.407 --> 00:03:20.700
The awareness is always there.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:20.909
Right.

00:03:20.909 --> 00:03:23.912
I would say to follow up on that, he's

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:25.580
really wonderful

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:28.458
at using human stories

00:03:28.458 --> 00:03:31.461
that are quite personal to reveal

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:34.547
and deal with bigger themes.

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:35.548
And and Shoni

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:36.883
Warfield and Erica Dunbar

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:38.343
were really instrumental

00:03:38.343 --> 00:03:39.636
in being able

00:03:39.636 --> 00:03:41.804
to create that nuance

00:03:41.804 --> 00:03:43.598
in the world that Peggy moves through.

00:03:43.598 --> 00:03:45.016
We were really able to collaborate

00:03:45.016 --> 00:03:48.311
with Julian in just adding those textures

00:03:48.311 --> 00:03:48.645
so that

00:03:48.645 --> 00:03:50.271
they don't feel

00:03:50.271 --> 00:03:52.982
quite like icing on the world, but still

00:03:52.982 --> 00:03:55.944
actually get to be rooted in their own

00:03:55.944 --> 00:03:57.111
rich internal lives.

00:03:57.111 --> 00:04:00.448
And so it was special to get to kind of

00:04:00.531 --> 00:04:02.575
alchemy is what that could be.

00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:04.494
There's a real there's a real wit.

00:04:04.494 --> 00:04:06.788
I think, to cutting from Peggy

00:04:06.788 --> 00:04:08.373
being in mortal danger

00:04:08.373 --> 00:04:09.165
to, you know,

00:04:09.165 --> 00:04:11.209
this is the soup going to be delivered

00:04:11.209 --> 00:04:12.835
without spilling.

00:04:12.835 --> 00:04:14.379
And I think there's a

00:04:14.379 --> 00:04:15.338
there's a temptation to see this

00:04:15.338 --> 00:04:16.673
as like two separate storylines.

00:04:16.673 --> 00:04:17.465
But actually,

00:04:17.465 --> 00:04:17.715
I think

00:04:17.715 --> 00:04:18.716
when you go from one to the other,

00:04:18.716 --> 00:04:19.008
you see,

00:04:19.008 --> 00:04:19.926
oh, this is

00:04:19.926 --> 00:04:21.135
this is what privileges,

00:04:21.135 --> 00:04:22.011
this is what wealth is.

00:04:22.011 --> 00:04:23.221
It creates a world

00:04:23.221 --> 00:04:23.888
where the stakes

00:04:23.888 --> 00:04:25.306
are just absurd

00:04:25.306 --> 00:04:27.016
as opposed to life and death.

00:04:27.016 --> 00:04:27.350
It's a

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:29.143
such a fascinating period,

00:04:29.143 --> 00:04:31.813
but it's also so like our own.

00:04:31.813 --> 00:04:34.399
It's there's a new class of people.

00:04:34.399 --> 00:04:36.442
Then it was industrial revolution money.

00:04:36.442 --> 00:04:38.528
Now it's tech money

00:04:38.528 --> 00:04:41.906
just bubbling up from, you know, from

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:43.658
seemingly from nowhere

00:04:43.658 --> 00:04:45.368
and challenging the status quo

00:04:45.368 --> 00:04:47.495
and so much disposable income.

00:04:47.495 --> 00:04:49.872
This is sort of a grotesque way

00:04:49.872 --> 00:04:52.333
African-Americans use striving

00:04:52.333 --> 00:04:56.087
for equality and immigrants

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:59.465
facing prejudice and maybe competition

00:04:59.549 --> 00:05:03.261
as they saw it with African-Americans and

00:05:03.344 --> 00:05:07.015
women trying to push the envelope and

00:05:07.098 --> 00:05:09.058
trying to have the right to vote,

00:05:09.058 --> 00:05:12.270
much less work or even have careers.

00:05:12.312 --> 00:05:13.688
It's certainly is

00:05:13.688 --> 00:05:14.856
about the fear

00:05:14.856 --> 00:05:15.815
that certain people

00:05:15.815 --> 00:05:17.483
certainly my character

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:19.444
feels about a world that is in,

00:05:19.444 --> 00:05:20.653
